<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Riau-Lingga Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1824 CE – 1911 CE · capital Daik (later Penyengat)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Malay sultanate of the Riau islands, a centre of Malay literature under its Bugis viceroys until the Dutch deposed its last sultan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> split the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor-Riau</a> realm. The mainland and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a> fell in the British sphere, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingga-islands">Lingga</a> islands stayed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> under a separate line of sultans based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daik">Daik</a> on Lingga, descended from the Bendahara dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Real power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-dipertuan-muda">Yang Dipertuan Muda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penyengat">Penyengat</a> island. Gambier and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> planted by Chinese settlers on Bintan funded the court. Penyengat became a cradle of Malay letters, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ali-haji">Raja Ali Haji</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurindam-dua-belas">Gurindam Dua Belas</a> and an early Malay grammar, laying foundations for standard Malay.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Growing friction with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> led them to depose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-rahman-muazzam-shah">Abdul Rahman Muazzam Shah</a> in 1911; he fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a> and the sultanate was formally abolished in 1913, its lands absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-malay">Riau Malay</a> later became the basis of Indonesian.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/riau-lingga-sultanate.jpg" alt="Riau-Lingga Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wakanebe Wizard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Riau-Lingga_Sultanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1824 CE – 1911 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Daik (later Penyengat)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdul Rahman Muazzam Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lingga-Riau, Sultanate of Riau-Lingga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=riau-lingga-sultanate" aria-label="Riau-Lingga Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7584 2141 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Riau-Lingga Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7904 2397L7881 2378M7904 2397L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7881 2378h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7904 2397h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7904 2397h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=riau-lingga-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
